<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1575 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hanuman Chalisa</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="awa">हनुमान चालीसा</bdi> / <bdi lang="awa-Latn">Hanumān Chālīsā</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Hindu hymn of forty short verses praising Hanuman, the strong and loyal helper of Rama. Millions say it every day.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Tulsidas</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (traditional melodies in Mishra Khamaj)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Hindus, devotees of Hanuman and Rama</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Awadhi</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Varanasi, India</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>16th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Tulsidas (16th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a>, &quot;forty chaupais on Hanuman&quot;, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> devotional hymn (stotra) in praise of Hanuman, written in Awadhi by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a>, a poet-saint of the 16th century. Tulsidas, devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, is best known for the Ramcharitmanas, his retelling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> in the Awadhi vernacular. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> until his death, founded the Sankat Mochan Hanuman Temple there, believed to stand where he saw Hanuman, and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> plays, folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of the Ramayana. Hanuman, a general of the vanaras and Rama's devotee and warrior in the war against Ravana, is worshipped as a deity in his own right, and in the Shaiva tradition as an incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>.</p><p>The name comes from chālīs, &quot;forty&quot;, the number of its chaupais (quatrains). With two introductory dohas and one closing doha the hymn has forty-three verses. The first doha honours the guru, understood as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, Hanuman's teacher. The first ten chaupais describe Hanuman's form, knowledge, virtues and bravery; the next ten his service to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, among them his part in reviving Lakshmana; then the poet turns to Hanuman's grace and ends by asking him to dwell in the hearts of his devotees, with Rama, Lakshmana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>. The hymn holds that whoever recites it gains protection, merit and even liberation.</p><p>Millions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> recite it every day, and many know it by heart; Tuesdays and Saturdays are traditionally Hanuman's days. Yet no commentary was written on it before the 1980s, which Rambhadracharya ascribes to its absence from printed editions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a>'s collected works. Indubhushan Ramayani wrote the first brief commentary, and Rambhadracharya's Mahaviri commentary in Hindi followed in 1983.</p><p>It is one of the best-selling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> religious books and has been sung by many devotional, classical and folk singers. Hari Om Sharan's recording, released in 1974 by the Gramophone Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and again in 1995, set to traditional melodies in the raga Mishra Khamaj, is played in temples and homes across northern India. A 1992 recording on the same melodies, sung by Hariharan with Gulshan Kumar, became in November 2025 the first devotional song and the first video on YouTube to pass five billion views. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/m-s-subbulakshmi">M. S. Subbulakshmi</a>, Pandit Jasraj and Bhimsen Joshi sang it, Amitabh Bachchan recorded it with twenty other singers in 2011, and the American singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> Das performs it in the West. Hindi films such as 1920 and Bajrangi Bhaijaan use it in key scenes.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise and petition addressed to Hanuman. It celebrates his strength, courage, wisdom and devotion, recalls his deeds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>'s service, and asks him for protection and to live in the devotee's heart together with Rama, Lakshmana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>. Its mood is confident, energetic and devout.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="10264190" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Jaydeep-poddar-hanuman-chalisa-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
